Deep neural networks (DNNs) are increasingly employed in cyber-physical systems (CPSs) to enable advanced perception and control capabilities. Hardware accelerators based on field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs) are increasingly used to accelerate DNN inference in real-time applications. FPGA-based heterogeneous platforms offer highly predictable timing behavior and low power consumption, making them well suited for embedded systems with stringent resource constraints. In such platforms, DNN inference can be efficiently executed by soft-core processors such as the AMD Deep Learning Processor Unit (DPU), which is specialized for accelerating common tensor operations in DNNs. This paper presents a scheduling and analysis framework designed to achieve time-predictable DNN inference on multi-core DPU configurations in FPGA-based systems on chip (SoCs). The framework provides timing guarantees by leveraging memory traffic regulation in the DPU inference and priority-driven scheduling of split DNNs to regulate access to the DPU cores. The effectiveness of the proposed approach is evaluated through an extensive experimental campaign on commercial AMD platforms, comparing its schedulability performance against standard techniques.